WEBVTT

00:01.180 --> 00:03.190
!!! REMEMBER TO ADD EDGEBLUR TO THE SUB TEXT, >>>>>>>>>NOT<<<<<<<<< THE LOGO/TS/OP/ED

00:01.680 --> 00:05.010
Paris. The end of the 19th century.

00:05.180 --> 00:08.810
Beneath the glass ceiling of the <i>Galerie du Roy</i>,

00:09.180 --> 00:14.860
one can see the thin silhouette of a Japanese girl named Yune.

00:15.610 --> 00:21.280
For now, only a few Parisians know of her arrival...

00:34.330 --> 00:37.800
Ikoku Meiro
no Croisée

00:34.330 --> 00:37.800
The crossing in a foreign maze

00:34.330 --> 00:37.800
Paris. The end of the 19th century.
Beneath the glass ceiling of the Galerie du Roy,
one can see the thin silhouette of a Japanese girl named Yune.
For now, only a few Parisians know of her arrival...

00:34.330 --> 00:37.800
The crossing in a foreign maze

00:37.800 --> 00:43.180
As I open the gate and step out into the street

00:43.800 --> 00:47.850
asa no hikari ga mabushii

00:43.800 --> 00:47.850
朝の光が眩しい

00:43.800 --> 00:47.850
I can see the sun shining brightly

00:50.850 --> 00:55.860
doko kara ka nagareru hanauta ni

00:50.850 --> 00:55.860
どこからか流れる鼻歌に

00:50.850 --> 00:55.860
I can hear humming in the distance

00:57.020 --> 01:00.780
kokoro hazumi tokete yuku

00:57.020 --> 01:00.780
心弾み解けてゆく

00:57.020 --> 01:00.780
It moves me and melts my heart

01:03.990 --> 01:09.450
mabuta tojireba ukabu egao

01:03.990 --> 01:09.450
瞼閉じれば浮かぶ笑顔

01:03.990 --> 01:09.450
I can see that smile when I close my eyes

01:10.620 --> 01:16.040
omoidasu tabi ni namida hitotsu

01:10.620 --> 01:16.040
思い出す度に涙ひとつ

01:10.620 --> 01:16.040
It brings me to tears whenever I remember it

01:16.540 --> 01:19.670
te wo hiroge hashiridase

01:16.540 --> 01:19.670
手を広げ 走りだせ

01:16.540 --> 01:19.670
Spread your arms out and take off

01:19.670 --> 01:23.010
hi no ataru toori wo

01:19.670 --> 01:23.010
陽の当たる 通りを

01:19.670 --> 01:23.010
Down the streets where the lights shine

01:23.010 --> 01:29.560
michiyuku hito ni genki ni aisatsu

01:23.010 --> 01:29.560
道行く人に 元気に挨拶

01:23.010 --> 01:29.560
Cheerfully greet people walking down the city streets

01:29.560 --> 01:36.190
atarashii kimochi de shinkokyuu shitara

01:29.560 --> 01:36.190
新しい 気持ちで深呼吸したら

01:29.560 --> 01:36.190
Take a deep breath to refresh yourself

01:36.190 --> 01:44.700
kono sekai wa hora itsudemo kimi no mono

01:36.190 --> 01:44.700
この世界はほら いつでも 君のもの

01:36.190 --> 01:44.700
And this world will always be yours

01:50.120 --> 02:00.090
Episode 03 :

01:50.120 --> 02:00.090
"Japanese Labyrinth"

02:15.390 --> 02:18.400
It's hot, you know. Try not to burn yourself.

02:18.980 --> 02:19.860
Okay!

02:30.280 --> 02:32.660
You're pretty good, Yune!

02:33.120 --> 02:35.000
Thank you very much.

02:35.000 --> 02:36.460
Isn't she, Claude?

02:40.250 --> 02:43.880
There are things similar to irons in Japan, too.

02:43.880 --> 02:48.220
They're called hinoshi, and they're used to straighten creases in kimono.

02:50.390 --> 02:52.890
Hey, what's Japan like, anyway?

02:53.220 --> 02:55.430
Japan?

02:58.020 --> 03:00.730
It's not really that difficult to answer, is it?

03:00.730 --> 03:05.780
Yune's hometown, Nagasaki, was like a labyrinth made of wood and paper.

03:05.780 --> 03:06.820
Yes, that's it!

03:06.820 --> 03:08.530
Wood and paper?!

03:08.530 --> 03:09.820
What do you mean?

03:10.660 --> 03:14.240
Japanese windows are made of paper.

03:14.240 --> 03:15.410
Yes!

03:15.410 --> 03:17.370
The doors and lamps are, too.

03:17.370 --> 03:18.080
Yes!

03:18.080 --> 03:20.960
Almost everything else is made of wood.

03:20.960 --> 03:24.840
Nagasaki had many short wooden
buildings that looked identical.

03:25.210 --> 03:30.590
It seemed like I was a giant who had walked into a land of dwarfs.

03:30.840 --> 03:34.510
The furniture was so small that they looked almost like toys,

03:34.510 --> 03:37.230
and the floors were carpets made of hay.

03:37.230 --> 03:38.850
A carpet of hay?!

03:38.850 --> 03:42.230
Indeed. They were marvelously woven.

03:42.230 --> 03:44.270
We would sleep on those at night.

03:44.520 --> 03:47.150
Must've been a tough life...

03:47.900 --> 03:51.410
Not at all. Yune lived in quite a grand mansion.

03:51.870 --> 03:55.030
You could see the Japanese-style
garden through a large window,

03:55.030 --> 03:58.330
which looked like a miniature of mountains and rivers!

03:58.910 --> 04:00.500
A miniature...

04:00.500 --> 04:03.880
That's right. It's where Yune is from, after all...

04:03.880 --> 04:05.750
Welcome to Japan!

04:07.800 --> 04:09.010
Welcome!

04:12.430 --> 04:17.640
Each house had a well of its own, with an endless supply of water!

04:18.770 --> 04:20.140
One for each household?!

04:20.140 --> 04:21.230
Endless supply?!

04:21.230 --> 04:25.320
They also had a sport where naked men would put dumplings on their heads,

04:25.320 --> 04:27.860
throw salt, and battle each other!

04:27.860 --> 04:29.240
That's sumo!

04:29.240 --> 04:31.320
That's right! Sumo!

04:31.610 --> 04:34.160
Naked men put dumplings on their heads,

04:34.160 --> 04:36.490
throw salt, and battle each other?!

04:38.200 --> 04:40.370
Japan seems like a horrifying country.

04:41.290 --> 04:43.790
I still can't get any inspiration from such a place.

05:05.810 --> 05:08.150
Did you get an order for a sign?

05:09.320 --> 05:12.110
He promised to buy it if it is to his liking.

05:12.860 --> 05:16.450
You've never accepted an order like that before.

05:17.410 --> 05:18.580
You think so?

05:18.950 --> 05:21.910
I don't think so, I know so!

05:21.910 --> 05:23.960
Well, I can't keep that up anymore.

05:24.620 --> 05:27.040
Are we really that desperate for business?

05:27.040 --> 05:29.170
Oscar-sama!

05:29.170 --> 05:31.420
I found it!

05:32.420 --> 05:33.930
What's this?

05:33.930 --> 05:37.600
That is what Japanese people use to eat.

05:37.600 --> 05:40.430
Wow, they really <i>do</i> look like toys.

05:41.270 --> 05:41.980
What's this?

05:41.980 --> 05:44.310
It's a container for sake.

05:45.480 --> 05:46.600
This thing?

05:47.270 --> 05:48.730
Hold this for a second.

05:48.730 --> 05:49.820
Okay.

05:52.280 --> 05:55.410
They fit pretty well in Yune's hands.

05:55.410 --> 05:58.700
But, your hands are really small.

05:59.330 --> 06:03.250
I'd be worried about breaking
things if everything was this small.

06:03.710 --> 06:06.000
They're sturdier than you think.

06:10.840 --> 06:16.130
Hey, if we go out of business, why don't we go to Japan?

06:16.130 --> 06:18.760
Don't joke about that.

06:18.760 --> 06:20.970
The <i>Galerie</i> won't go out of business.

06:20.970 --> 06:23.100
I won't allow that.

06:27.310 --> 06:28.810
How wonderful!

06:28.810 --> 06:33.650
The exquisite feel of the silk, and this bold, flowing design!

06:34.280 --> 06:40.160
This kimono you gave to me was easily the best birthday present I received this year!

06:41.280 --> 06:44.290
You really like Japan, don't you, Alice-chan?

06:44.290 --> 06:46.830
On the other hand, Father just...

06:46.830 --> 06:51.590
The only thing he did was promise me ownership of the <i>Galerie du Roy</i>!

06:51.920 --> 06:54.380
Oh? What's so bad about that?

06:54.380 --> 06:58.550
Brother received the <i>Grand Magasin</i> as well.

06:59.050 --> 07:02.140
But the <i>Grand Magasin</i> is popular!

07:02.140 --> 07:04.310
I just got the <i>Galerie du Roy</i>!

07:04.310 --> 07:07.480
It's a rotten shopping district running itself into the ground!

07:07.480 --> 07:10.730
We might as well just tear it down and erect a hotel!

07:11.020 --> 07:15.360
Well, you know, I bought your kimono in that rotten place!

07:18.240 --> 07:21.530
Is there really such a fancy store there?

07:30.370 --> 07:35.250
Is there anything else that needs ironing, Claude-sama?

07:35.250 --> 07:36.510
Nope, it's fine.

07:38.260 --> 07:39.590
I see.

07:51.270 --> 07:53.230
Want to take a break?

07:55.270 --> 07:56.150
Yes!

08:00.030 --> 08:05.200
Oh yeah, you never told me the real reason you came here.

08:05.200 --> 08:06.830
I heard that you would be a live-in maid,

08:06.830 --> 08:09.750
but you didn't have to come all the way to Paris to do that.

08:10.670 --> 08:14.590
I've always admired Paris.

08:14.590 --> 08:19.010
I was interested in many things, so I asked Oscar-sama,

08:19.010 --> 08:21.220
and he granted my wish.

08:21.220 --> 08:24.100
You asked him personally?

08:24.100 --> 08:25.050
Yes.

08:25.050 --> 08:28.600
Don't you want to go out and
see more of Paris, then?

08:29.270 --> 08:32.060
You haven't looked around the <i>Galerie</i> at all.

08:32.400 --> 08:37.570
I still don't know where I want to go or what I want to see.

08:37.570 --> 08:42.110
She still needs to get used to living here.

08:42.110 --> 08:43.660
Right, Yune?

08:43.660 --> 08:45.120
I'm sorry.

08:46.330 --> 08:48.330
You shouldn't be apologizing for that.

08:49.330 --> 08:53.120
Anyway, do you want to try out some new food, Yune?

08:59.260 --> 09:00.800
It looks amazing!

09:00.800 --> 09:02.760
It's a pastry called <i>baba</i>.

09:02.760 --> 09:04.930
It doesn't just <i>look</i> amazing.

09:04.930 --> 09:06.180
Have a bite.

09:13.100 --> 09:14.270
Here, you do it like this.

09:25.820 --> 09:27.990
So you cry for delicious things, too...

09:27.990 --> 09:30.160
Seems like you enjoyed it.

09:30.160 --> 09:32.160
W-Wait!

09:32.160 --> 09:36.670
Could I have that paper as well?

09:43.840 --> 09:45.470
Origami?

09:45.470 --> 09:47.680
Some people in France do that, too.

09:50.720 --> 09:53.560
This is amazing.

09:57.100 --> 09:58.650
It's a crane.

09:58.650 --> 10:02.990
Aren't <i>you</i> the magician, Yune?

10:02.990 --> 10:05.240
It's my way of saying thanks for the <i>baba</i>!

10:21.550 --> 10:25.010
Dearest sister, I hope that everything is going well.
Yune has gone overseas, to Paris, with Oscar-sama
and his grandson Clau

10:23.670 --> 10:25.010
What are you writing?

10:25.010 --> 10:26.510
Wh-What?

10:26.510 --> 10:27.680
Uh-

10:27.680 --> 10:28.800
N-N-No!

10:28.800 --> 10:29.350
Don't!

10:29.350 --> 10:30.550
Don't read it!

10:30.550 --> 10:32.890
Japanese, huh...

10:32.890 --> 10:33.770
Dearest sister, I hope that everything is going well.
Yune has gone overseas, to Paris, with Oscar-sama
and his grandson Clau

10:33.390 --> 10:35.890
They don't even look like letters.

10:41.730 --> 10:44.650
How do you write your name, Yune?

10:44.650 --> 10:46.820
My name?

10:48.070 --> 10:49.160
Yu...

10:49.160 --> 10:49.240
Yune

10:49.160 --> 10:51.740
...ne.

10:51.740 --> 10:54.040
Does it have another meaning?

10:54.040 --> 10:56.120
"Yu" means hot water,

10:56.120 --> 10:59.580
and "ne" means sound.

10:59.580 --> 11:02.170
Yune means "The sound of hot water."

11:02.170 --> 11:04.210
The sound of hot water...

11:16.060 --> 11:20.270
Japanese people believe that the sounds of nature are beautiful,

11:20.270 --> 11:21.610
and are relaxed by it.

11:21.610 --> 11:28.110
I'm Yune, and my older sister is "The sound of an ebbing tide,"

11:29.490 --> 11:29.610
Shione

11:31.660 --> 11:33.780
You have an older sister?

11:33.200 --> 11:33.330
Shione

11:33.780 --> 11:34.580
Yes.

11:35.950 --> 11:39.580
Are you writing a letter to your sister, then?

11:39.580 --> 11:40.580
Yes.

11:40.580 --> 11:44.460
You can't send a scrap of paper like this to Japan.

11:45.380 --> 11:47.670
You need to buy some proper paper.

11:48.420 --> 11:51.800
We used to have a stationery store in the <i>Galerie</i>, but...

11:51.800 --> 11:54.260
No, that isn't a letter!

11:54.260 --> 11:58.100
Also, proper paper is very expensive.

11:58.560 --> 12:00.350
I can't afford that luxury.

12:00.730 --> 12:03.860
What are you so worked up about all of a sudden?

12:07.570 --> 12:09.360
I'll be taking this.

12:46.730 --> 12:47.730
Here you are.

12:49.440 --> 12:51.280
What a cute bird.

12:51.280 --> 12:54.410
It's a crane; a Japanese bird of celebration.

12:54.870 --> 12:57.410
Let us celebrate our encounter.

13:04.710 --> 13:05.630
Here we go.

13:06.000 --> 13:06.920
Here you are.

13:06.920 --> 13:08.170
Thank you!

13:15.050 --> 13:16.100
Yune.

13:18.390 --> 13:19.390
How's this?

13:20.310 --> 13:21.640
A sign?

13:24.440 --> 13:26.310
It's "sound!"

13:26.310 --> 13:27.230
Yep.

13:28.230 --> 13:30.570
I used the letters in your name.

13:31.030 --> 13:32.150
Isn't it nice?

13:33.990 --> 13:34.910
Yes!

13:36.660 --> 13:38.280
I'll go deliver this.

13:39.660 --> 13:41.660
Keep an eye on the store, all right?

13:41.950 --> 13:42.830
All right!

14:18.990 --> 14:20.080
Grandpa!

14:21.950 --> 14:23.410
Oh, Claude!

14:23.410 --> 14:25.120
Good timing.

14:25.120 --> 14:29.000
Could you head back home soon? I left Yune alone at the store.

14:29.590 --> 14:31.550
Did something happen?

14:33.960 --> 14:35.300
I sold the sign.

14:35.300 --> 14:36.550
Really?

14:37.010 --> 14:41.470
What? Am I really that bad at selling stuff?

14:41.470 --> 14:44.640
No, that's great!

14:44.640 --> 14:47.650
Y-Yeah. I'm gonna go get some letter paper for Yune.

14:58.360 --> 14:59.530
A cat!

15:10.080 --> 15:12.130
Oh, but Claude-sama said...

15:12.130 --> 15:14.590
<i>Keep an eye on the store, all right?</i>

15:15.970 --> 15:17.840
I can't break my promise!

15:18.260 --> 15:19.590
But...!

15:28.560 --> 15:29.520
It's raining.

15:34.570 --> 15:35.480
Yune!

15:36.190 --> 15:37.650
Oscar-sama!

15:37.650 --> 15:39.110
Welcome home!

15:39.110 --> 15:41.450
Were you able to manage the store by yourself?

15:41.910 --> 15:42.700
Yes!

15:43.990 --> 15:45.290
Wait, how did you know?

15:45.540 --> 15:47.910
I ran into Claude in town.

15:47.910 --> 15:50.710
He was talking about buying you letter paper.

15:55.130 --> 15:55.710
Yune!

16:01.470 --> 16:04.390
Claude-sama didn't bring an umbrella!

16:04.390 --> 16:07.430
Please watch the store, Oscar-sama.

16:10.770 --> 16:14.270
Rain doesn't last too long in Paris.

16:24.530 --> 16:27.490
What are you looking for, Sir?

16:28.830 --> 16:34.590
I'd like to send a letter overseas.
Do you know what kind of paper a girl would use?

16:34.590 --> 16:36.630
Let's see...

16:37.460 --> 16:40.340
How about this blue paper?

16:40.670 --> 16:43.680
This color has become very popular lately.

17:28.810 --> 17:30.430
Claude-sama!

17:30.430 --> 17:32.560
I'm surprised you didn't get lost out here.

17:35.100 --> 17:39.570
Did you buy paper for me, Claude-sama?

17:39.980 --> 17:41.650
Geez, that old man...

17:41.650 --> 17:45.030
I wanted it to be a surprise.

17:45.570 --> 17:46.780
I-I didn't hear anything!

17:46.780 --> 17:49.160
Oscar-sama didn't say anything!

17:51.290 --> 17:54.040
But, thank you!

17:54.040 --> 17:56.250
I should be the one saying that.

17:57.080 --> 17:59.380
I sold that sign!

17:59.380 --> 18:01.170
Really?!

18:03.340 --> 18:04.590
Oh, here's your umbrella!

18:07.800 --> 18:08.850
What's this?

18:08.850 --> 18:10.470
It's the sound of the rain.

18:10.470 --> 18:11.470
The rain?

18:11.470 --> 18:14.810
Japanese people really enjoy the sounds of nature.

18:14.810 --> 18:17.730
In turn, we also enjoy the sound of rain falling on an umbrella.

18:25.950 --> 18:27.280
I suppose it's kind of nice.

18:28.780 --> 18:31.450
Do you mind taking a bit of a detour, Yune?

18:31.450 --> 18:33.500
But, the rain...

18:33.500 --> 18:35.540
Rain doesn't last too long in Paris.

18:36.000 --> 18:37.620
Really?!

18:44.880 --> 18:45.590
Here.

18:48.930 --> 18:50.760
<i>Passage du Caire</i>.

18:50.760 --> 18:53.310
The Egyptian goddesses watch over it.

19:39.940 --> 19:41.560
You were right.

19:41.560 --> 19:43.820
The rain stopped very quickly.

19:46.990 --> 19:47.820
Look, Yune.

19:53.160 --> 19:54.580
Wow!

19:55.120 --> 19:56.950
A sunset in Paris.

19:57.830 --> 20:01.750
I love the view from this bridge.

20:02.920 --> 20:06.670
The sky hasn't changed since I was little.

20:07.670 --> 20:09.090
Claude-sama...

20:11.050 --> 20:13.640
I really wanted to show this to you.

20:22.900 --> 20:26.480
Japanese people ring bells, too, Claude-sama.

20:26.480 --> 20:28.650
We do it at the temples where we worship gods.

20:28.650 --> 20:30.700
That's the same in the churches in Paris!

20:30.700 --> 20:34.370
Japanese bells are different from the ones in Paris, though.

20:34.370 --> 20:35.740
They go,

20:35.740 --> 20:37.290
Gong~!

20:37.290 --> 20:38.750
Gong~!

20:38.750 --> 20:40.080
Dong~!

20:40.080 --> 20:41.420
Dong~!

20:41.420 --> 20:42.370
No!

20:43.290 --> 20:44.960
Gong~!

20:44.960 --> 20:47.380
Gong~!

20:51.630 --> 20:53.340
What's the difference?

21:01.480 --> 21:04.350
The sound of Paris.

21:19.040 --> 21:25.630
aa marude yume wo miteru you na

21:19.040 --> 21:25.630
ああ、まるで夢を見てるような

21:19.040 --> 21:25.630
Ah, it's as if I'm in a dream

21:25.630 --> 21:31.420
yawarakana hikari no naka de

21:25.630 --> 21:31.420
柔らかな光の中で

21:25.630 --> 21:31.420
As I lie here in this tender light

21:31.420 --> 21:42.430
me wo tojite mimi wo sumasereba kikoete kuru

21:31.420 --> 21:42.430
目を閉じて耳を澄ませれば　聞こえてくる

21:31.420 --> 21:42.430
When I close my eyes and listen, I can hear it

21:44.270 --> 21:57.320
atatakaku watashi wo yobu yasashii sono koe ga

21:44.270 --> 21:57.320
あたたかく私を呼ぶ優しいその声が

21:44.270 --> 21:57.320
That kind voice that warmly calls to me

21:59.910 --> 22:06.500
afureru omoi wo tsutaetai kono te kara

21:59.910 --> 22:06.500
溢れる思いを伝えたい　この手から

21:59.910 --> 22:06.500
I want to extend my feelings with these hands

22:06.500 --> 22:12.800
kotoba ja nakute kokoro de tsunagaru

22:06.500 --> 22:12.800
言葉じゃなくて　心で繋がる

22:06.500 --> 22:12.800
We connect through our hearts, not our words

22:12.800 --> 22:19.470
hajimeyou koko kara sodateyou nukumori wo

22:12.800 --> 22:19.470
始めよう　ここから育てよう　温もりを

22:12.800 --> 22:19.470
Let's begin here. Let's nurture the warmth

22:19.470 --> 22:25.940
atarashii hibi ga watashi wo matteru

22:19.470 --> 22:25.940
新しい日々が私を待ってる

22:19.470 --> 22:25.940
A new day is waiting for me

22:25.940 --> 22:39.660
mamoru mono wa kitto koko ni aru no

22:25.940 --> 22:39.660
守るものはきっとここにあるの

22:25.940 --> 22:39.660
I'm sure there is something here for me to protect

22:44.500 --> 22:46.670
Hey, Alice-chan.

22:47.040 --> 22:48.040
What?

22:48.040 --> 22:51.880
Why don't you take a look around
the <i>Galerie du Roy</i> some time?

22:51.880 --> 22:54.550
You may be able to appreciate it more.

22:55.220 --> 23:01.100
I've also heard rumors that there have been sightings of a Japanese girl there.

23:01.100 --> 23:02.810
What did you say?!

23:03.220 --> 23:04.310
Edgar?!

23:04.310 --> 23:05.640
Edgar?!

23:07.810 --> 23:09.060
How may I be of service?

23:09.060 --> 23:12.520
Find the Japanese girl in the <i>Galerie du Roy</i>!

23:12.520 --> 23:14.940
I wish to have tea with her!

23:14.940 --> 23:16.240
Yes, ma'am.

23:16.240 --> 23:17.530
Yes!

23:17.530 --> 23:22.740
Just wait, <i>Japonaise</i>-san! I'll find you in no time!

